# Build output.
#
# The binary name is anchored with a leading slash so it matches only the built
# artifact at the repository root. Unanchored, "go-netmon" also matched the
# directory cmd/go-netmon/ and silently kept the poller's own main package out
# of the repository - CI caught it as "directory not found" on the first build.
/go-netmon
*.exe
*.dll
*.so
*.dylib

# Compiled test binaries from "go test -c". The negation matters: this pattern
# also matches any file whose name ends in ".test", and it was excluding
# test/integration/Dockerfile.test — so CI checked out a tree with no dockerfile
# for the integration runner and failed with "open Dockerfile.test: no such file
# or directory" while every local run worked from the untracked copy on disk.
*.test
!Dockerfile.test
!**/Dockerfile.test
/vendor/
# Modules vendored on the host for the image builds (scripts/docker-vendor.sh)
/.docker-vendor/
/bin/
/dist/

# IDEs and editors
.idea/
.vscode/
*.swp
*.swo
*~

# OS
.DS_Store
Thumbs.db

# Review tooling scratch.
#
# The Serena indexer that the local review tooling activates writes .serena/
# into the repository it is reviewing and does not clean up after itself — a
# review round was observed creating it and then being blocked by its own
# sandbox from removing it, so it comes back every round. Its inner .gitignore
# covers only /cache and /project.local.yml, leaving .serena/.gitignore and
# .serena/project.yml exposed to any broad `git add`. Nothing from it has been
# committed here only because commits stage explicit paths, which is a habit
# and not a guard.
.serena/

# Local configuration.
#
# config.yaml is this deployment's own settings and is bind-mounted into the
# containers. config.example.yaml is the committed template; copy it to
# config.yaml on a fresh checkout. Device credentials (SNMP communities, v3
# auth) are held in the web UI's store, not here, but this file still describes
# one host's deployment rather than the project.
config.yaml
# deploy/config.yaml is the opposite: it is the tracked starting configuration
# shipped to a deployment target, and it holds no credentials because devices
# live in the web UI's store. The rule above matches at every level, so it has
# to be exempted by name or `make deploy` fails on a clean checkout.
!deploy/config.yaml
*.local.yaml
.env
.env.local

# Logs
*.log
logs/

# Coverage and profiling output.
#
# Scoped to the coverage artifacts by name. An unqualified "*.html" was here
# before and silently excluded internal/webui/static/index.html — the entire web
# UI page the server serves — from the repository.
*.coverprofile
*.out
coverage.html
coverage-*.html
/coverage/

# Runtime state, not source.
#
# data/ holds the local (non-Docker) run's Pebble stores; backups/ holds
# netmon-backup archives, which are hundreds of megabytes of compressed volume
# copies and are themselves the disaster-recovery artifact, not source.
/data/
/backups/

# Node dependencies for the Playwright e2e suite, restored by npm ci.
node_modules/
/tests/e2e/test-results/
/tests/e2e/playwright-report/

# Scraped vendor support lists. These are saved HTML pages from Cisco's site
# used to check which objects a platform implements; they are reference
# material, not project source, and are large.
mibs/*/support-list/
missing-mibs.txt

# The MIB trees under mibs/ are testdata and are committed.
#
# They were briefly excluded as "operational, belongs with the infrastructure
# config" and that was wrong: the parser and dashboard suites read them
# directly through internal/testsupport.MIBDir, so a clean checkout could not
# run its own tests. They are also the project's only multi-vendor parser
# coverage - mibs/asr1001 is Cisco IOS-XE and mibs/r640 is Dell OpenManage, and
# the Dell modules (10892.mib, dcstorag.mib) exercise SMIv2 constructs the
# Cisco set does not.
#
# Only the scraped support-list pages above are excluded; they are reference
# material no test reads.
